== Imitation Learning, Distribution Shift, and Offline-to-Online Reinforcement Learning

Behavioral cloning (BC) is the simplest form of imitation learning, reducing policy learning to supervised regression on expert demonstrations. Ross and Bagnell @ross2010reduction established foundational error bounds showing that BC suffers $O(T^2 epsilon)$ regret under covariate shift, where $T$ is the horizon length and $epsilon$ is the per-step classification error. Because the learner never encounters its own mistakes during training, small errors compound quadratically as the policy drifts into states absent from the demonstration distribution. DAgger @ross2011dagger addresses this by iteratively querying the expert under the learner's induced state distribution, but requires repeated access to an oracle policy. De Haan et al. @dehaan2019causal further demonstrated that BC is susceptible to _causal confusion_, where the learned policy latches onto spurious correlations in the demonstration data rather than the true causal features governing expert behavior. Laskey et al. @laskey2017dart proposed DART, which injects calibrated noise into demonstrations during data collection, forcing the learner to observe recovery behavior and thereby building robustness to the distributional shift that standard BC ignores.

The transition from a BC-pretrained policy to on-policy RL fine-tuning introduces a distinct set of failure modes beyond those present in pure imitation learning. Wolczyk et al. @wolczyk2024forgetting showed that _catastrophic forgetting_ is the primary mechanism behind poor BC-to-RL transfer, as gradient updates from the RL objective rapidly overwrite the representations learned during pretraining. Nikishin et al. @nikishin2022primacy identified a related phenomenon they term _primacy bias_, in which neural network representations formed during early training phases become entrenched and resist subsequent adaptation, explaining why BC-pretrained features may lock in suboptimal representations that the RL phase cannot escape. Rajeswaran et al. @rajeswaran2018dexterous proposed a practical mitigation for robotics, augmenting the RL objective with a decaying BC auxiliary loss that gradually releases the policy from the demonstration distribution while preventing catastrophic performance collapse during the transition.

A separate line of work has developed principled offline-to-online RL methods that avoid the sharp transition our BC-to-PPO pipeline exhibits. Nair et al. @nair2020awac introduced AWAC, which uses advantage-weighted regression to seamlessly incorporate offline demonstration data into the online learning loop, avoiding the distribution mismatch that arises when on-policy methods like PPO discard prior data entirely. Kostrikov et al. @kostrikov2022iql proposed IQL, which circumvents out-of-distribution action evaluation by learning value functions through expectile regression over the dataset support. Ball et al. @ball2023calql introduced Cal-QL, which calibrates pessimistic Q-value initialization to prevent the _valley of despair_ phenomenon wherein an agent's performance drops precipitously before recovering during the offline-to-online transition. Uchendu et al. @uchendu2023jsrl proposed Jump-Start RL, in which a pretrained guide policy controls early episode timesteps and progressively cedes authority to the RL policy as it matures. These methods collectively suggest that the BC-to-PPO collapse we observe arises from multiple interacting factors. Compounding covariate shift @ross2010reduction pushes the policy off the demonstration distribution, on-policy optimization discards the stabilizing demonstration buffer @nair2020awac @ball2023calql, pretrained representations resist adaptation @nikishin2022primacy, and the value function must bootstrap from scratch without calibrated initialization @ball2023calql. Off-policy algorithms with demonstration replay offer a principled solution but require architectural departures from the on-policy PPO framework.
